# The laws of the algebra of types, over kinds of which no type says how they are related: a class without an instance test, a class and one that extends it, functions and classes of functions.
# Nothing is ever lost: an intersection and a cast hold whatever lies in both, a difference whatever the other lacks, a union both, and what overlaps meets somewhere.
# Five laws do not hold here: no type says that an object is an instance of two classes, so an intersection names both classes, or the lower one of a class and one that extends it, and a difference is an upper bound.
# An intersection is the same both ways and a cast has its values, so four of the five are broken equally often.
# How often each is broken may only shrink, and never at the price of a law that holds.
# RUN: laws-of-types
# CHECK: laws of types: 21 of 21 types
# CHECK-NEXT: sum MeetLiesInFirst: 144 times in 144 shapes
# CHECK-NEXT: sum MeetLiesInSecond: 144 times in 144 shapes
# CHECK-NEXT: sum CastLiesInFirst: 144 times in 144 shapes
# CHECK-NEXT: sum CastLiesInSecond: 144 times in 144 shapes
# CHECK-NEXT: sum DifferenceMeetsSecondNowhere: 200 times in 200 shapes
# CHECK-NEXT: 21 of 26 laws hold

